  "title": "Custom iterable of dirs",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "md": "## `for...of` calls `[Symbol.iterator]`\n\nAn object is iterable if that method returns `{ next() { return { value, done } } }`.\n\nBuild a tiny iterable that yields `\"east\"`, `\"east\"`, `\"south\"` so the fox can `for (const d of path) Player.move(d)`."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "id": "for-of-needs",
      "promptMd": "`for (const x of obj)` requires…",
      "kind": "mcq",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "array",
          "md": "That obj is an Array"
        },
        {
          "id": "iter",
          "md": "That obj has Symbol.iterator"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "iter"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "id": "iterable-path",
      "engine": "javascript",
      "entry": "main.js",
      "promptMd": "> Make `path` iterable: east, east, south. Walk to (2, 1).",
      "files": [
        {
          "path": "files/main.js",
          "role": "edit"
        },
        {
          "path": "files/hidden.test.js",
          "role": "hidden-test"
        }
      ],
      "checks": [
        {
          "type": "js-assert"
        }
      ],
      "hintLadder": [
        {
          "level": 1,
          "kind": "concept",
          "md": "You can also use dirs[Symbol.iterator] from an array. A custom next() is the lesson."
        },
        {
          "level": 4,
          "kind": "assist",
          "md": "const dirs = [\"east\", \"east\", \"south\"]\nconst path = {\n  [Symbol.iterator]() { return dirs[Symbol.iterator]() }\n}\nfor (const d of path) Player.move(d)\nmodule.exports = { path }"
        }
      ],
